\p{\strong{4:21 PM}}
\hr

\p{Simplified I/O Device Protocol: requires three "registers". One is the status register that tells the OS whether the device is ready or currently performing an operation. The second is the command register, which tells the device what we want to do. Lastly, is the data register that stores the actual data we want moved. The procedure operates as follows \citet{36.3}{arpaci2018operating} }
\ol{
  \li{OS polls status register in while loop until \code{status == READY}}
  \li{OS writes data register with data to be moved}
  \li{OS writes command register with command to be performed}
  \li{Device begins command and sets \code{status = BUSY}}
  \li{OS polls status register in while loop until \code{status == READY}}
}

\p{\strong{4:33 PM}}
\hr

\p{The above protocol requires that the CPU polls the device. This is a huge waste if the device is very slow because the CPU is unable to do any work while waiting. One solution is to use [interrupts](K325). In particular, the CPU follows the above procedure but after writing the command it resumes doing other work. Upon completion, the device issues an interrupt to the CPU to tell it that it has finished the command and that the CPU can resume working on that line of instructions.}

\question{How is copying memory to device actually done in programmed I/O? Is the device address space mapped into the CPU address space and the CPU has 1. load from CPU address space to register, 2. write to device address from register?}

\answer{There are two ways. The first is there are special I/O instructions (typically protected) that allow the CPU to directly write to the device. In x86, these are \code{in}, \code{out}. The second way is through memory-mapped I/O (MMIO). This is where the device is actually allocated a region of virtual address space and writing to / reading this region is routed to the device rather than the on-chip memory hierarchy \citet{36.6}{arpaci2018operating}.}

\p{However, there is another issue with the above protocol. The CPU must spend work copying all data to the device before it can hand control over to the device. The CPU managing data copy is called programmed I/O (PIO) Due to this, we now have the DMA (direct memory access engine). The DMA is provided with the address of the desired data to be copied in DRAM, and then is able to manually copy the data from DRAM to device completely asynchronously.}

\p{Lastly, we do not want our operating system to have to handle every possible device. We instead want to utilize \em{abstraction} to allow the OS to handle a wide range of devices through some common interface. These are what are now commonly known as \em{device drivers}. This is essentially an ABI. One example is that the driver has to coordinate between the CPU and device, where the CPU will actually write the dispatching instruction.}

\question{Is the instruction the CPU writes uniform across device and it is the drivers job to translate?}
\answer{Yes. Each device can have its own register layout i.e. different registers correspond to different things. In the case of NVMe, this is actually standardized in the NVMe specification, so all NVMe devices can use the same driver.}
